No, you shouldn't. Steeds are a pain in the rear end. 

- you can't use a ring of conflict without being chucked off your
mount;
- liches keep cursing the saddle, which means you can't get off
(without a great deal of trouble);
- you can't pick stuff up from the ground unless you're basic or more
in riding, which takes up valuable skill slots;
- horses keep dying because there's never anything to feed them;
- dragon steeds can't use their special attacks when you're riding
them;
- and last but not least, all steed monsters have a much worse AC and
# of hit points than you do by late game. This means you can take
about ten times the damage your steed can, which invariably means it
ends up dying. Even if you have a handy /oUT in your inventory, you
still have to zap the corpse, (possibly) retame the monster, pick up
that stupid heavy saddle and put it back on your steed, and get back
on. Over and over and over...until you give up in frustration.

> Being able to fly rocks, and it effectively halves 
> the damaage you take as enemies will often attack your steed and not 
> you. 

That's the problem!

> It also means you can save a wish on your boots, because the 
> special boot effects don't work while riding. Warhorses have speed 24, 
> faster than you can get even with speed boots (which is 18).    
> Jabberwocks hit by a wand of speed have speed 18, I think.

And when the monster dies and you're left standing by its corpse,
surrounded by enemies and with no source of extra speed, you'll regret
not having spent that wish after all. (If you had pointed out it saved
wishing for boots of water walking or levitation, I might be more
inclined to agree with you.)

I really tried keeping a steed during my last ascension. Honest. I
wanted to soar to victory on my trusty red dragon, striking down
enemies effortlessly as I flew through the air. I tried. But after
good old Firebolt died for the tenth time (as did Firestorm and
Tornado, after him) I gave up. It's not worth it. If you must fly, put
on some DSM and drink a potion of polymorph.

